# Signed native TEST procedure integration

The exact surviving saved candidate now completes actual native signed TEST
execution. The authoritative completed source profile is `profiles/v4`; the
unchanged run is `results_attempt4`. Its independent retained-artifact audit is
`native_test_integration_attempt4_audit_v1.json`.

The run executes 136 fresh, prospectively frozen inputs: 32 test, 24 adversarial,
48 held-out and 32 shadow inputs. All complete correctly through the native
interpreter's six handlers: 13 true decisions and 123 false decisions. False is
a computed answer, rather than an operational failure. There are 816 native
handler dispatches, 408 distinct current-invocation check receipts and 136
distinct emitted decision receipts. The audit restores all 408 native trace,
outcome and invocation-receipt objects, checks their exact CID joins and verifies
all three check receipts at postcondition and final validation. The 136 original
invocation bodies are exactly reconstructed from retained query/authority records
and checked against their original native CIDs; this reconstruction is separately
retained, rather than described as an original checkpoint archive.

Thirty pre-issuer evidence negatives and thirteen native/runtime controls pass.
The latter include forged/expired/catalog-mismatched certificates, LIVE denial,
changed observations, cross-invocation observations, substituted query
descriptors, changed query bytes, swapped invocation bindings and out-of-domain
inputs. Exact invocation checkpoint reuse succeeds without redispatch within
the same interpreter process. Cross-process checkpoint restoration is not tested.

Eight separate evidence artifacts precede native verification and signing. The
proof checks seven bounded logical decision contracts in Z3 and bridges them to
4,100 concrete source executions. Separate concrete test/adversarial/held-out/
shadow results are independently recomputed against an oracle before native
`AdmittedReceipt` values are created. Native seven-layer verification, certificate
issuance and certificate admission all pass. The proof is a bounded logical
operation-contract result plus a measured source bridge; it is not an arbitrary
Python theorem. The exact saved IR has an empty required-proof-contract list,
which remains unchanged. The adapter enforces the named proof payload; this is
not native contract-specific proof coverage.

Concrete float adversaries remain included. Native canonical values reject
floats, so the v4 transport commits exact canonical JSON text and exposes a typed
query-CID descriptor to native values. Each handler checks descriptor identity,
decodes canonical bytes and resolves the exact invocation-bound query before
calling the original catalog. Across the complete evidence corpus, 1,192 inputs
contain floats. Original training and exposed inputs are excluded from all five
new partitions; these are author-constructed inputs prepared after development,
not a population generalization test.

| Charged stage | Wall seconds |
| --- | ---: |
| Preparation and prospective freeze | 1.402121482 |
| Fresh evidence producer process | 1.521004141 |
| Independent artifact reader, controls, native verifier and issuer process | 6.902932652 |
| Native TEST runtime and controls process | 8.819003316 |
| Independent retained-artifact audit | 2.929697514 |

Each original worker was bounded at 120 seconds and exited zero. The three
worker times total 17.242940109 seconds; preparation adds 1.402121482 seconds.
The 136 native prepare-and-execute calls total 6.771036383 seconds inside the
runtime process. The evidence controls consume 5.127013646 seconds inside the
verification process. These nested values must not be added again. Artifact
admission repeats the concrete evidence evaluations, and runtime observation/
validation recomputes catalog results; this experiment does not establish
receipt-only execution or computational savings. Handler-reported integer
millisecond costs cover less than the complete runtime, so enclosing measured
times are the accounting boundary. Immutable source-profile copying before the
driver and the small v3 serialization diagnostic were separate preparation work;
profile-copy wall time was not instrumented, and the diagnostic's measured time
is retained in `proof_smt_identity_diagnostic_v3.json`.

Evidence object storage is 2,373,459 bytes in 12 objects, including the task-family
and three example bodies. Native runtime object storage is 2,704,141 bytes in
2,998 objects. These are payload-storage counts, excluding source snapshots,
corpora, logs, summaries and the additive audit.

The prospective source manifest pins 10,736 paths. Actual module-import receipts
record 156 loaded file paths; every actual native source is in the freeze and
matches its hash. The audit lists 112 observed standard-library or other loaded
dependency paths outside the native source freeze. The Python executable is
pinned, but an observed module receipt is not complete transitive shared-library
closure.

Earlier attempts remain unchanged. V1 stopped during evidence production because
native CID serialization rejects floats; this was a transport limitation, not a
procedure decision failure. V2 preserved float input bytes but stopped while
serializing native immutable mappings. V3 produced evidence but rejected repeated
SMT serialization because Z3's context-local auxiliary names changed. V4 uses
fresh solver contexts and lossless mapping normalization. Original bounds,
failures, source profiles, process costs and freezes for all attempts are retained
in the audit. No previous failure was overwritten or treated as a timeout.

This is a bounded sequence-decision integration with a TEST-only signer and
trusted local immutable-input custody. The local reservation adapter does not
provide OS isolation against hostile Python. The runtime selection receipt is
explicitly unpromoted; no native registry promotion, production authority,
repository-repair generalization, provider call or model-token saving is claimed.

